      "sequence_position": 3,
      "problem": "If $\\frac{{p}^{2}}{5}+15=0,$ then $p=$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we rearrange the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{p}^{2}}{5}+15 & =0 \\\\ \\frac{{p}^{2}}{5} & =-15 \\\\ {p}^{2} & =-75\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy taking square root, we obtain:\n\n$$\np=\\pm \\sqrt{-75}\n$$\n\nWe now rewrite $p$ using the imaginary unit $\\text{i}=\\sqrt{-1}\\,:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p & =\\pm \\sqrt{-75} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{75\\cdot (-1)} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{75}\\cdot \\sqrt{-1} \\\\  & =\\pm 5\\sqrt{3}\\cdot \\sqrt{-1} \\\\  & =\\pm 5\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
